```

This is the function that's used to create a new DisputeGame proxy contract, now as hinted by the @audit tag, there is a check to ensure the game has not been created before, whereas this is a good check, it would lead to an issue in an edge case.



The user creates a new DisputeGame by calling `DisputeGameFactory.create`. Creating a new DisputeGame requires the user to provide `_gameType`, `_rootClaim` and `_extraData (l2BlockNumber)`. These 3 parameters constitute the UUID of this DisputeGame. If the UUID is already in use, the `create` fails.

Issue with this approach is that every DisputeGame is a "game" that could _fail_ even if we have a correct _rootClaim_. Now once the game that contains the correct `rootClaim`  fails and since this UUID can no longer be used, a new DisputeGame cannot be started with the correct `rootClaim`. Which results in that withdrawals from this `rootClaim` will not be executed.

Note that the above is a viable scenario, albeit a bit low likelihood, cause consider a case when no one defends against challengers. It would become necessary to create a new DisputeGame with the correct rootClaim to enable users to withdraw. However, the DisputeGameFactory contract does not allow the creation of a DisputeGame with the same _gameType, _rootClaim, and _extraData due to [this check](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-optimism/blob/2e11e15e7a9a86f90de090ebf9e3516279e30897/packages/contracts-bedrock/src/dispute/DisputeGameFactory.sol#L116-L127).




## Impact

Valid withdrawals would be blocked.

## Tool used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Consider allowing a recreation of the same game if the dispute fails for a specific `UUID`.



